//Que: Count the words present in given string
//Program
public class Main
{
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		String s1=" nagpur is in maharashtra 123 ";
		String[] s2=s1.trim().split(" ");
		if(s1.isEmpty()) {
			System.out.println("Empty string");
		}
		else {
			System.out.println(s2.length);
		}
	}
}

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Approach: 
  1. Problem Understanding:
The goal is to count the number of words in a string, where words are separated by spaces.
Edge cases to consider:
Leading and trailing spaces.
Multiple spaces between words.
Empty string.

2. Issues in the Current Code:
The split(" ") method splits the string based on single spaces, but it doesn’t handle multiple spaces correctly.
For example, "a b" (with 3 spaces) will result in ["a", "", "", "b"], and s2.length will incorrectly count empty strings as words.
The isEmpty() check is redundant because trim() ensures the string is not empty unless it was originally empty.

3. Optimized Approach:
Use split("\\s+") to split the string based on one or more spaces.
Handle edge cases like empty strings or strings with only spaces.

Time and Space Complexity:
1. Time Complexity:
O(n), where n is the length of the string.
trim(): Takes O(n) to remove leading and trailing spaces.
split("\\s+"): Takes O(n) to split the string into an array.
isEmpty() and s2.length: O(1) operations.

2. Space Complexity:
O(n), where n is the length of the string.
The split() method creates an array (s2) that stores the words, which takes O(n) space.

Optimized code:

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s1 = " nagpur is in maharashtra 123 ";
        String[] s2 = s1.trim().split("\\s+"); // Split by one or more spaces
        if (s1.trim().isEmpty()) { // Check if the string is empty after trimming
            System.out.println("Empty string");
        } else {
            System.out.println(s2.length); // Print the number of words
        }
    }
}
